\pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs22\lang9\u8239?...Meanwhile the hour of\u8239?noon\u8239?drew on, and\u8239?waked\u8239?\par
\par
This is the most fundamental moment in human history for Milton\rquote s readership in 1667 \f1\endash  the pivotal moment in History takes place, appropriately at the pivotal moment in the day. This captures neatly the idea that we are at a telling \ldblquote hour\rdblquote  for humanity. Milton draws our attention to the specific time of day in order to foreground the idea that Time is, in a sense, going to be radically different hereafter.\f0\par
\par
An eager appetite,\u8239?raised\u8239?by the smell\u8239?\u8239?\tab\tab\tab\tab 740\u8239?\u8239? \par
So\u8239?savoury\u8239?of that fruit, which with desire,\u8239? \par
Inclinable\u8239?now grown to touch or taste,\u8239? \par
Solicited\u8239?her longing eye; yet first\u8239? \par
\par
Milton employs a lexical field that prepares us for her later \ldblquote rash\rdblquote  decision. The language is weirdly sexualised \f1\endash  this fits the early modern prejudice that women were deficient in irrationality and were, essentially, slaves to their bodily \ldblquote appetites\rdblquote . Eve as a representative woman is desirous of sensual experience, greedy, \ldblquote needy\rdblquote . \f0\par
\par
Pausing a while, thus to herself she\u8239?mused. \par
\par
Great are thy\u8239?virtues, doubtless, best of fruits.\u8239?\u8239?\tab\tab 745\u8239? \par
Though kept from man, and worthy to be\u8239?admired,\u8239? \par
Whose taste, too long\u8239?forborne, at first assay\u8239? \par
Gave elocution to the mute, and taught\u8239? \par
The\u8239?tongue not made for\u8239?speech to speak thy praise:\u8239? \par
Thy praise\u8239?he\u8239?also who forbids thy use,\u8239?\u8239?\tab\tab\tab 750\u8239? \par
\par
Despite the negative portrayal of Eve in this passage, here she seems to express a sentiment which is somewhat reminiscent of Milton's own relationship with religion, until it again becomes tainted. She asks why something so praised by all, both a simple creature and the one who commands her, is forbidden from her, removing Eve's ability to know- Milton despised organised religion for this very reason. However her direct address to the tree with 'thy' begins to reiterate a less noble portrayal, as she is already communicating (unlike the serpent who was 'mute'), craving more than she perhaps needs, ignoring an order out of desire. This applies to the character of Eve throughout Paradise Lost, as one who acts out of shallow emotions, even if with modern interpretations it may be a fight for equality, the same experiences as the serpent and God.\par
\par
Conceals\u8239?not from us, naming thee the\u8239?tree\u8239? \par
Of knowledge, knowledge both of good and evil;\u8239? \par
Forbids us then to taste, but his forbidding\u8239? \par
Commends thee more, while it\u8239?infers\u8239?the good\u8239? \par
\par
The demand that God makes with regard to the forbidden fruit itself makes the fruit attractive. It as at moments like this \f1\endash  when Milton explores fundamental aspects of human psychology \endash  that Paradise Lost is often at its most powerful as a text.\par
William Blake, a romantic critic, supported this view. He viewed Gods prohibition of the forbidden fruit without an explanation, made him a 'tyrant'. He views that God creates the prohibition knowing the temptation it will cause. This was the opposite of Milton's intent of the christian epic, which aimed to 'justify the ways of God to men' rather, Blake argues '(Milton) was of the devil's without knowing it'.\f0\par
\par
By thee communicated, and our want:\u8239?\tab\tab\tab\tab 755\u8239? \par
For good unknown, sure is not had, or had\u8239? \par
And yet unknown, is as not had at all.\u8239? \par
In plain\u8239?then, what forbids he but to know,\u8239? \par
Forbids us good, forbids us to be wise?\u8239? \par
Such prohibitions\u8239?bind\u8239?not. But if death\u8239?\tab\tab\tab 760\u8239? \par
Bind us with after-bands, what profits then\u8239? \par
Our inward freedom? In the day we\u8239?eat\u8239? \par
\par
Here Eve equates having knowledge and freedom. Their awareness of good and evil will \lquote free\rquote  them from the ignorant prelapsarian state, and free from the authority of God. \par
\par
Of this fair fruit, our doom is, we shall die.\u8239? \par
How\u8239?dies\u8239?the serpent?\u8239?he\u8239?hath\u8239?eaten\u8239?and lives,\u8239? \par
\par
In her questioning of God and what she had been told, Eve has already started to sin. By considering Satan's words, Eve appears to have lost any chance of resisting temptation. \par
\par
And knows, and speaks, and reasons, and discerns,\u8239?\tab\tab 765\u8239? \par
Irrational till then. For us alone\u8239? \par
Was death invented? or to us\u8239?denied\u8239? \par
\par
This question is a great contrast to the previous comment of Eve, where she imagines a freedom in the serpent's speaking, elaborating upon the notion of it as the ability to 'reason' and 'discern'- the constant stream of verbs suggest such a fixation on this subject, only exaggerating the envy of this statement. As the only people on Earth, her and Adam are the only ones able to die, bringing into question the morality of God for allowing tragedy in their lives, something critics like Blake and Shelley observe. However, Eve's thoughts are far from heroic, as she is about to doom humanity, further highlighting the strange justification of God's actions from Milton, regardless of the extreme cruelty key to his character (such as the excessive labour needed in the garden, to 'tend, plant, herb and flower').\par
\par
This intellectual food, for beasts\u8239?reserved?\u8239? \par
For beasts it seems: yet that one beast which first\u8239? \par
Hath tasted, envies not, but brings with joy\u8239?\tab\tab\tab 770\u8239? \par
The good\u8239?befallen\u8239?him,\u8239?author\u8239?unsuspect,\u8239? \par
Friendly to man,\u8239?far\u8239?from deceit or guile.\u8239? \par
What fear I then, rather what know to\u8239?fear\u8239? \par
\par
Romantic critics, and indeed almost every critic of the poem post-Romanticism, has viewed Eve\rquote s lack of \ldblquote fear\rdblquote  and her notable curiosity, as admirable. Eve\rquote s defiance of God\rquote s Word was perceived by Blake, Wordsworth, Shelley and Empson as worthy of commendation not condemnation.  \par
The Duchess of Malfi is also a character who notably does not show fear when faced with her own death. This is made clear through the juxtaposition of her calm manner with Cariola's panic.\par
\par
Under this ignorance of good and evil,\u8239? \par
 \par
Of God or death, of law or\u8239?penalty?\u8239?\tab\tab\tab\tab 775\u8239? \par
Here grows the cure of all, this fruit divine,\u8239? \par
\par
Eve\rquote s hyperbolic language reveals that all is not well here: she is under the influence of Satan and therefore her judgements are not be trusted, have gone awry.\par
\par
Fair to the eye, inviting to the taste,\u8239? \par
Of\u8239?virtue\u8239?to make wise: what hinders then\u8239? \par
To reach, and feed at once both\u8239?body\u8239?and mind?\u8239?\u8239? \par
\par
 \par
\par
So saying,\u8239?her rash hand\u8239?in evil hour \tab\tab\tab\tab 780\u8239? \par
\par
Here, Eve's actions are viewed as 'rash'. It was an Early Modern essentialism that impulsivity was fundamental to female nature. Women were viewed as irrational despite Milton's use of Eve's internal voice between the lines 745-774. Eve in her internal voice questions and explains her decision to eat the forbidden fruit but the narrative voice maintains the view that she is acting without thought. Milton's description of her 'rash hand' is therefore a reflection of Early Modern misogynistic attitutes towards women. The hostility of men towards women originates from this moment, therefore Milton creates man and woman to be  fundamentally distinct. Adam is an intellectual, while Eve is feeble-minded, for Milton, this justifies male Early Modern actions towards women.\par
\par
Forth reaching to the fruit, she \u8239?plucked, she eat:\u8239? \par
\par
Eve's voice appears almost completely coherent in her reasoning as to whether she should eat the fruit, but the narrative voice states her hand is "rash". To a 21st century reader, this is not "rash" but the logical thing to do given God's orders are given on the pretence of blind faith. \par
\par
What is arguably the most pivotal, fundamental moment in all of human history (the Fall of Man) is reduced to a mere four words. Eve's actions are presented to us in their most simplistic form: 'She plucked, she eat'. This seems an odd choice, given how descriptive the rest of the passage is, yet perhaps it is successful in fully emphasising the importance of the moment: there is no other way to describe it, as overly wordy language would strip away its importance. \par
Also, it seems we must remember the words used here - we have to remember the action taken word-for-word because of its sheer importance... and more importantly that it was Eve's 'fault' ('She' is used twice in quick succession to enforce this).\par
I love the point that the succinct expression makes it memorable: this is really important given the didactic function of the poem - we must remember this moment so that we avoid the vices of Eve (willfulness, disobedience) and Adam (uxoriousness, a lack of assertiveness).  \par
\par
Earth felt the wound, and nature from her seat\u8239? \par
Sighing through all her works gave signs of woe,\u8239? \par
That all was lost\'85(Paradise Lost, IX.739-784) \par
